nging from pathways that  are more or less core to the human  microbiome they must be present in every  individual in some environment sometimes  rarely these are provided by a specific  organism and that organism is the same  between individuals but more commonly  there's diversity in which organisms can  provide a particular type of  biochemistry so here's an example in  which only one organism per individual  tends to provide a pathway but that  organism can differ between individuals  and then finally more typically there  can be many different microbes per  individual that can carry out a  particular type of biochemistry and  often it's not even the same bugs per  individual so there can be diversity  both in which organisms per individual  provide a pathway and in how they change  between individuals so this leads us to  some of the challenges in microbial  community in human microbiome  analysis and data science which range  all the way from the very molecular  focus that I was just describing up to  higher-level questions of population  health and microbial interactions I  highlighted already a lot of the dark  matter or open questions around the  underlying biochemistry or cellular  biology that we can see in the  microbiome from these different types of  molecular data and of course many of  these affect translation and human  health as I mentioned the beginning the  microbiome provides not just a high  dimensional readout of human health but  also a potential point of intervention  once we know the right direction in  which to push it we can make changes to  a microbial community in a way that it  would be very difficult with human  genetics finally many of these human  health considerations have effects at  the population level as well so for  example early life antimicrobial or  probiotic exposures later then influence  microbiome acquisition or development  and thus later life allergic or atopic  phenotypes as well so all the tools that  I briefly mentioned today are available  online as part of the labs bio bakery  collection both of individual  open-source methods and a virtual image  that includes all of these pre-built and  documented and set up within a single  environment so if you're interested in  in follow-up work using any of these  methods take a look at the bio bakery  site and I'd like to thank all of those  faces that you saw going by who the  people who actually did the work either  in the lab or several of our  collaborators much of this work was was  with from Xavier at the broad I  mentioned Owen and new markers group at  the University of Maryland wendy garrett  here at the School of Public Health and  then several former lab members such as  Nicola Sagada who has since gone on to  carry on a great deal of this work on  the earth their own so with that I'll  wrap up again think that the organizer  the audience and I think there's a  little time left for questions Curtis  this was a fantastic talk thank you so  much for sharing this with us this is  really I've got about 4,000 question  and in five minutes to ask them in um  but I wanted to just these are some  questions of my own and as somebody who  is not a microbiome assist this is  fascinating for me and when I back in  the olden days when I was learning about  genetics there was a real emphasis at  least kind of during the initial phases  of the the micro biological genomics  kind of explosion there was still a lot  of interest in doing population genetics  and looking at things like gene fitness  and as genes propagate through a  distribution and whatnot is there any  sort of in your opinion opportunities  for those old-school  population geneticist mathematicians to  take a look at some of these data and  look at gene frequencies in some of  these different populations and and what  happens to those genes as they spread  through populations and either are  beneficial or otherwise does that make  sense it makes sense it's a great  question and very timely one one of the  things I think has been exciting about  the past years worth of computational  biology in the microbiome is the ability  to see that kind of data at sufficient  resolution to do sort of classical  microbial genetics in a culture  independent or metagenomic way so some  of these strain identification methods  for example has only just recently  become precise enough to track strain  variation and divergence and population  genetics for every bug in a community at  the same time and we're starting to get  to the ten thousands of gut microbiome  samples available globally to do that  sort of tracking across multiple  populations simultaneously so yeah that  is yeah it's so exciting because you  know when you're talking about your  population genetics at least you know  the Cavalli sports and Bodmer type of  old-school population genetics you know  that was all like very theoretical right  lots of differential equations to try to  theoretically analyze what would happen  Jeane spread through a population to  fixation and hardy-weinberg equilibrium  xand so forth and so on but you actually  have the population of bugs to analyze  and watch what happens as a new gene  gets introduced into that population and  what happens to it and be able to watch  the behavior of the bugs watch their  biochemical output as a consequence of  as these genes spread through that  population what happens to them and is  that beneficial or not and I was also  thinking about something that you had  mentioned about the specificity of the  different bugs for their various niches  on the body where you have say you know  something that lives on the skin is not  necessarily the same population of bugs  which live inside the gut and I was also  curious about because it looked like you  were sort of talking about I don't know  if you touched on it in particular but  immune responses to when say something  it's even from an individual something  like bugs from their gut get introduced  accidentally into a different area of  the body so I was thinking in my head  like what happens when because you know  for those of us who've had kids what  happens when bugs from the gut get  introduced into the eye this is  basically pinkeye right so you basically  get gut bugs introduced into the  conjunctiva of the eye and you get an  immune response of swelling and pus and  all sorts of other nastiness and you  know there's a really dramatic and  obvious immune response can you imagine  being able to look at the role of the  immune system in all of this definitely  it's much more I should say when Wendy's  area of expertise and then mine but we  work with her and andronik on immune  interactions quite a bit in in the gut  the kind of example that you raise come  comes up in the context of gut barrier  leakiness and the degree to which again  in a sort of cyclical way post triggered  inflammation can allow microbial  penetration through through the gut and  then conversely microbial invasion can  trigger inflammation and sustain that  leaking  so that certainly is a factor in  inflammatory bowel disease systemically  there is this interest and we've worked  with folks like Dan Littman on  rheumatoid arthritis we're certainly  either leakiness or perturbation in the  gut or potentially circulation or  triggers from distal microbes like oral  microbes could play a factor in sort of  miss triggering the immune system  systemically and then having it target  things like like the synovium at joints  in rheumatoid arthritis it's really  fascinating and one final question is  can you give us a comment on microbial  transplantation and what sort of  horizons exist for that well it's a  great question and you know every  everyone likes to talk about transplants  at the at the end of a talk  I'll not exact so there's there's I I  would certainly put the fecal microbiome  transplants in one of the real  translational success stories about the  microbiome so for so far certainly for  Clostridium difficile there's been a lot  of great results in properly screened  and clinically controlled the  transplants being able to really  ameliorate recurrent c-diff and they're  starting to be more work in FM tees for  inflammatory bowel disease where the  ecology is harder to restore again we  don't necessarily know yet what the  right pushes to give back immunity to  get it back to stability within an  individual and there's a lot of  subjective subject variation so I think  some of the neatest work going on in  that space right now is in understanding  one what is the best push to give to say  an ulcerative colitis individual what's  the right group of microbes whether that  should come from an FM key or whether  you can make an artificial cocktail that  behaves like an FM t and then to how to  tailor that to each individual so that  no matter what their sort of current or  baseline microbiome is that push gets  them back to a healthy state and that's  where  things like against train tracking and  understanding the biochemistry or  functional profile of what these bugs  should be doing is it helpful well with  that I'd like to thank Curtis Sutton how  her from Harvard Medical School or  School of Public Health excuse me for  his fantastic talk I'm just so just  blown away about a still another several  thousand questions but we've run out of  time but Thank You Curtis that was  really fantastic and thanks to everyone  who joined us today for those who've  been asking this is being recorded and  will be available online here a little  later today so if you want to rewatch it  or share it with friends please by all  means do and once again let me thank you  for tuning in to the big data to  knowledge guide to the fundamentals of  data science please look forward to  future lectures that we'll be having  here in the coming weeks thanks again  have a great weekend everybody
 

